schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: "Wikipedia preserves deleted-page text so it can be studied later"
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    Wikipedia almost never truly destroys content: old page versions live in
    page history, talk-page discussions get moved to dated archive subpages
    rather than erased, and even deleted pages keep their text in the
    database — as one editor put it, "in 100 years when historians want to
    study this incident, they can."
  evidence: >-
    Cited in the article from Wikipedia's "Help:Page history" and
    "Help:Archiving a talk page" help pages, listed in the article's
    References.
  interpretation: >-
    Used as the canonical large-scale example of "archive means history, not
    trash" — the article argues page history is institutional memory that
    lets a thousand strangers trust the current version of a page.
  uncertainty: >-
    The attributed editor quote is reproduced as given in the article,
    without a named editor or a direct link to the specific discussion it
    came from; not independently verified for this ingestion.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/archive-compost.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-d6e9e6e15257
  source_lineage: archive-compost.md
